  "guide": {
    "features": [
      {
        "icon": "play",
        "text": "PWM speed and direction pins: how the duty cycle sets the motor voltage and two inputs set its direction."
      },
      {
        "icon": "board",
        "text": "Stall current: a blocked wheel draws far more than a spinning one."
      },
      {
        "icon": "tip",
        "text": "Battery sag under load and the Uno brown-out."
      }
    ],
    "hints": [
      "The robot resets as soon as the motors start: look at the battery voltage while the motors start. A weak pack sags and the Uno browns out.",
      "The robot veers or turns the wrong way: one motor runs backward. Check the left motor leads.",
      "One wheel does not turn and the current is high: the motor is stalled, so look for something blocking the wheel. A stalled motor draws about 1 A here.",
      "The robot stops or the Uno resets when both motors start: compare the motor current with the driver limit.",
      "The left wheel spins and the robot drifts: the wheel is slipping, so look at the grip under it.",
      "Still stuck? Compare each reading with the run before you changed anything."
    ],
    "kind": "challenge",
    "lede": "A two-wheel robot that drives forward, turns a quarter turn and stops, on your code alone.",
    "level": "project",
    "overview": "No sensors yet. The Uno sets a direction on two pins and a speed on a PWM pin for each motor, and the TB6612FNG driver turns that into current from the battery. Learn the driver first; the sensors come later.",
